      "claim": "Propranolol, a related aryloxypropanolamine, showed oral bioavailability of 54 to 79 percent in cirrhotic patients.",
      "card_ids": ["C05"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "All ten visible L1 contexts report measured oral bioavailability values at or above 20 percent, with no context supporting a low outcome.",
      "card_ids": ["C01", "C03", "C05", "C10", "C12", "C15", "C19", "C25", "C28", "C32"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "current_gold_voting_records",
      "applicability": "high",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C01", "C03", "C05", "C15", "C19", "C25", "C28", "C32"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct measured oral bioavailability exists for the query molecule.",
    "No L2 records were supplied to clarify first-pass metabolism or formulation effects.",
    "The urea substituent on the query phenyl ring has no exact matched comparator among the visible contexts.",
    "Cirrhosis-conditioned records may not fully transfer to a general population.",
    "The physicochemical prior is mixed and low confidence due to ionization and polarity."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "The query is an aryloxypropanolamine beta-blocker analogue with a tert-butylamino group and a para urea substituent. All visible L1 gold records report oral bioavailability above 20 percent, including structurally close beta blockers metoprolol, timolol, and propranolol with values from 50 to 84 percent. No affirmative evidence supports F below 20 percent. The physicochemical prior is mixed but not threshold-relevant negative evidence. Therefore the positive class is better supported and the prediction is high with moderate confidence."
}

